(project borge
 (doc "A spec-driven code orchestrator")
 (status in-progress)
 (convention ocaml-dune
 )
 (roerick
  (|
  I named this project after Victor Borge, my favorite orchestra conductor. 
  the agent said .borg as the file extension was non-negotiable, 
  and I guess that's true. 
  |)
 )
 (section program-architecture
  (doc "How borge is structured: one root spec, inlined sub-specs, no orphans")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The root borge.borg file is the architectural contract. It
        declares what modules exist and how they relate by inlining
        their specs. It is the AGENTS.md equivalent for borge projects.

        (convention ocaml-dune) declares the structural rules for this
        project. Borge loads the rule set and uses it for structural drift
        detection. The convention covers: lib/ owns domain logic, bin/ owns
        CLI IO, dune files define libraries and executables, public_name
        conventions, test/ structure. Other conventions: go-standard,
        c-makefile, etc. This way lib.borg describes WHAT the lib does,
        not HOW OCaml works — the convention handles the how.

        Every .borg file must either be inlined by a parent or declare
        no-inline itself. A .borg file with no parent and no no-inline
        declaration is orphaned — this is structural drift.

        The inline directive is by filename: (inline cli.borg) means
        the file cli.borg is a sub-spec of this project. The parent owns
        the architectural relationship. The child owns the detail.

        Drift detection covers three layers:
          1. Spec drift: .borg file describes something not in the code
          2. Code drift: code implements something not in .borg
          3. Structural drift: code organization violates the convention
             (e.g. domain logic in bin/ when ocaml-dune says lib/ owns it)
      |))
  )
 )
 (section modules
  (doc "The modules that make up borge, inlined by filename")
  (status implemented)
  (subsection lang
   (doc "The borge language: parsing, printing, and balancing")
   (status implemented)
   (inline sexp.borg
   )
  )
  (subsection lib
   (doc "Domain operations: checking, reporting, formatting, spec extraction")
   (status implemented)
   (inline lib.borg
   )
  )
  (subsection cli
   (doc "CLI entrypoints: thin wrappers that call lib functions")
   (status implemented)
   (inline cli.borg
   )
  )
  (subsection history
   (doc "Change tracking and provenance for .borg files")
   (status implemented)
   (inline history.borg
   )
  )
  (subsection meta
   (doc "Companion metadata files for structured analysis observations")
   (status in-progress)
   (inline meta.borg
   )
  )
 )
 (section format
  (doc "The file format")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Verbatim strings use open-pipe to open and pipe-close to close.
        The parser tracks nesting depth. Content between the opening
        delimiter and the final closing delimiter that returns depth
        to 0 is v_content. The closing delimiter is NOT included in
        v_content - it is a delimiter, not content. Nested delimiters
        inside the content ARE included verbatim.

        The inline directive: (inline filename.borg) declares that the
        named file is a sub-spec of this section. The file must exist and
        be parseable. The parent owns the architectural relationship;
        the child owns the detail. Inline is by filename, relative to the
        parent file.

        The no-inline declaration: (no-inline) in a project form means
        this project has no sub-specs. This is the legitimate standalone
        case. Every .borg file must either be inlined by a parent or
        declare no-inline. Any other .borg file is orphaned.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section tool-pipeline
  (doc "Defensive toolkit for writing .borg files")
  (status implemented)
  (subsection balance
   (doc "Check paren balance. Run FIRST after every edit.")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
          Balance is a fast character-level scanner that tracks paren nesting
          depth. It skips verbatim strings, quoted strings, and comments so
          only structural parens are counted. It also validates that
          structural `)` on their own line align with their matching `(` .
          It is the #1 defensive tool: run it after every edit to catch
          structural errors before parse.
        |))
   )
  )
  (subsection balance-verbose
   (doc "Show paren stack on error: which ( matches which )")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
          When balance finds an unexpected close-paren, it reports the
          full paren stack: which open-paren is still open, at what line/col,
          and what keyword it belongs to. It also reports indent_mismatch
          errors when a structural `)` on its own line does not align with
          its matching `(` .

          With --repair, balance infers missing or extra `)` from indentation
          and outputs a corrected file with properly indented new lines.
          The --verbose flag adds the raw paren stack list.
        |))
   )
  )
  (subsection parse
   (doc "Full syntax validation. Run after balance passes.")
   (status implemented)
  )
  (subsection report
   (doc "Show status summary across all .borg files. Use this to plan work.")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
          CURRENT DRIFT: report treats all .borg files as peers and
          counts statuses independently. It does not walk the inline
          tree. This means inlined sub-specs are double-counted: if
          borge.borg says (subsection sexp (status implemented)) and
          sexp.borg also has its own statuses, both get counted. The
          fix: report should start from the root file, follow inline
          directives, and count each section once.
        |))
   )
  )
  (subsection future
   (doc "Show what's planned but not yet implemented - the roadmap view")
   (status planned)
   (depends-on
    "report command implementation"
   )
   (details
    (doc (|
          borge future scans all .borg files and extracts a roadmap:
          what's spec'd as 'planned' or 'in-progress' vs what's
          'implemented'. This is the forward-looking companion to
          'borge report' which shows current state.

          Output format:
          
          === PLANNED ===
          victor.borg:485  (section agentic-task-ui) - agent run popup UI
          victor.borg:523  (section agentic-task-ui) auto-accept mode
          borge.borg:320   (subsection doc-coverage-command)
          
          === IN PROGRESS ===
          victor.borg:237  (section codemirror-integration)
          
          === READY FOR REVIEW ===
          victor.borg:120  (subsection job-panel) - jobs complete
          (anything implemented but depends on planned items)

          Usage:
          borge future              # Show all planned work
          borge future --section X  # Future work in specific section
          borge future --summary    # Just counts: planned/in-prog/implemented

          This command helps answer:
          - "What's next on the roadmap?"
          - "How much of the vision is implemented?"
          - "What depends on what?"
          - "Which sections are ready to work on?"

          Integration with Victor IDE:
          - Shows in job panel as "12 items planned"
          - Click to see full future report
          - Can queue agents to work on planned sections
        |))
   )
  )
  (subsection nodes
   (doc "Dump the parsed AST with types and positions for debugging")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
          Walks the AST and prints each node with its type, keyword (if
          list), source position, and child count. Example output:
            1:1  List "project" [5 children]
              1:9  Atom "borge"
              1:16 List "doc" [2 children]
          This exposes what the parser actually sees - critical for
          debugging mismatches between source and AST.
        |))
   )
  )
  (subsection lint
   (doc "Semantic validation: invalid status, illegal placement, literacy.")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
          borge lint checks for:
          - Invalid status values (not planned/in-progress/implemented/etc)
          - Duplicate project names across .borg files
          - Orphaned .borg files (no inline parent, no no-inline)
          - Unknown comment types in annotated comments
          - Missing values in annotated comments
          - Deleted human-authored comments (compared to git HEAD)
          - Pending ask/response pairs without answers
          - (no-inline) on root files (root files can't inline anything)
          - Mechanical code quality: unsafe stdlib calls, polymorphic
            equality. borge lint --mechanical to enable.

          borge lint --literacy checks .ml files for:
          - Missing literacy scores on agent-written doc comments
            (no [NNNN] after the authorship tag)
          - Plausible scores: if STORY >= 6 the comment must show
            narrative context; if EXPLAIN >= 6 it must walk through
            mechanics; if TEACH >= 6 it must contain a transferable
            principle. Catches inflated scores like [7777777] on a
            2-line comment.
        |))
   )
  )
  (subsection drift
   (doc "Detect spec drift, code drift, and structural drift")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
          Three layers of drift detection:

          1. Spec drift: a .borg file describes something not in the
             codebase. E.g. cli.borg says (status implemented) for a
             command but no corresponding binary exists.

          2. Code drift: the codebase has something not in any .borg
             file. E.g. a .ml file exists but nothing in the specs
             mentions it.

          3. Structural drift: the code organization does not match the
             inline architecture. E.g. lib.borg says it owns Check and
             Report, but those modules live in bin/ instead. The root
             borge.borg says (inline lib.borg) but the actual dune
             library structure contradicts what lib.borg describes.
        |))
   )
  )
  (subsection fmt
   (doc "Canonicalize indentation. Run before commit.")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
          Parses and re-prints with canonical indentation. Children of
          keyword forms (project, section, subsection, details) go on
          their own lines. Atom names stay inline with keyword.
          (doc ...) and (status ...) always print inline. Verbatim
          strings preserve their content verbatim.
        |))
   )
  )
  (subsection fmt-check
   (doc "Dry-run: exit 1 if formatting would change the file")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
          borge fmt --check: parse the file, format it, compare to
          original. Exit 0 if identical, 1 if different. For CI
          pre-commit hooks. Also: borge fmt --diff shows the actual
          diff between current and formatted output.
        |))
   )
  )
  (subsection check
   (doc "Recursive health check across all .borg files.")
   (status implemented)
  )
  (subsection worst-failure-modes
   (doc "What goes wrong and which tool catches it")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
          Missing open-paren: caught by balance.
          Extra close-paren: caught by balance.
          Verbatim not closed: caught by balance (context tracking).
          Annotated comment inside form body: caught by parse (syntax).
          Invalid status value: caught by lint (semantics).
          Duplicate project name: caught by check (cross-file).
          Bad indentation: caught by fmt (auto-fixes).
          Tracking progress: borge report shows what is done and what is next.
          Formatter producing unbalanced output: caught by fmt --check
            or by running borge balance on fmt output.
          Orphaned .borg file: caught by drift (structural).
          Domain logic in wrong layer: caught by drift (structural).
          Human comment deleted by agent: caught by lint (comment integrity).
          Undocumented function: caught by drift (code-documentation).
          Unsafe stdlib call (List.hd, List.assoc, Hashtbl.find,
            String.sub, int_of_string, open_in, input_line):
            caught by lint --mechanical. Full rule table in
            lib.borg section check / subsection code-quality.
          Polymorphic equality on strings/lists: planned —
            lint --mechanical with AST walker.
          Deeply nested match (> 3 levels): planned — lint
            --mechanical with AST walker. Exception: state-
            machine patterns (e.g., balance checker).
        |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section comment-integrity
  (doc "Protect human-authored comments from silent agent deletion")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Annotated comments with a human author (e.g. (* roerick note ... *))
        are first-class spec content. Agents must never delete them silently.

        For planning agents (borge plan): the agent edits .borg files in
        .borge.lock/working/. At commit time, borge checks whether any
        human-authored comments were deleted by comparing working/ with
        original/. If deletions are found, the commit is blocked. The
        user must either restore the deleted comments or run borge commit
        --force to override. The diff original/..working/ is shown.

        For implementing/fixing agents (borge make, borge drift --fix):
        these agents cannot modify .borg files, so this check does not
        apply. Code file comment integrity may be added in the future.

        borge lint (legacy) compares the working tree against git HEAD.
        If a human-authored comment is present in HEAD but missing in the
        working tree, lint emits a deletion warning. This still works for
        non-agent editing but is redundant when the agent workflow is used.

        borge normalize is the human saying "I made changes on purpose."
        It runs borge fmt, then accepts all comment deletions as
        intentional by recording the current state as the new baseline.
        After normalize, lint will not warn about those deletions again.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section code-documentation
  (doc "Crawl .ml files for documentation coverage under ocaml-dune convention")
  (status in-progress)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Under the ocaml-dune convention, borge drift checks code files
        too. For every .ml file it finds all let bindings (functions
        and values). A binding is documented if it has a borge-style
        annotated comment above it:
          (* author note (|description of what this does|) *)

        Undocumented bindings are reported as a coverage gap. This makes
        literate programming the default: functions should explain
        themselves. To exempt a binding from documentation:
          (* exempt doc *)

        The drift report shows: total bindings, documented count,
        undocumented count, coverage percentage. This is code drift
        by another name: the code exists but the spec (in the form of
        inline documentation) does not cover it.
      |))
  )
  (subsection doc-coverage-command
   (doc "borge drift --docs: analyze documentation coverage")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       Implementation: borge drift --docs flag triggers documentation
       coverage analysis.

       Algorithm (implemented in lib/doc/):
       1. Find all .ml files (respecting _build/.git exclusion)
          via Doc_extract.find_ml_files
       2. Extract all top-level let bindings via
          Doc_extract.extract_bindings
       3. For each binding, check for preceding doc comment via
          Doc_detect.extract_file_docs:
          - (** docstring *) (ocamldoc style)
          - (* author note (|...|) *) borg-style comment
          - (*| ... |*) alternative borg format
          - (* exempt doc *) — marks as exempt, not undocumented
       4. Skip bindings (handled by Doc_extract classification):
          - let%test (test bindings)
          - let _ = (ignored bindings)
          - Bindings starting with _ (implicitly private)
       5. Output: (documentation ...) block in .borg.meta
          via Doc_coverage.generate_meta_block

       Coverage calculation (Doc_coverage.calculate_file_coverage):
       - documented = count of bindings with valid doc
       - exempt = count of bindings with (* exempt doc *)
       - total-bindings = all extracted bindings (after filtering)
       - undocumented = total - documented - exempt
       - coverage-percent = documented / (total - exempt) * 100

       Drift findings produced:
       - undocumented-module: file with coverage < threshold
       - undocumented-function: specific binding missing doc (if --verbose)

       CLI output (non-json):
       lib/borge/spec.ml: 38/45 documented (84.4%), 2 exempt, 5 undocumented
       lib/borge/parse.ml: 12/28 documented (42.9%) ⚠ undocumented-module

       Integration with stats: borge stats aggregates all (documentation ...)
       blocks from .borg.meta files for project-wide coverage report.

       Remaining work:
       - Wire Doc_coverage into borge_drift.ml as --docs flag
       - Add --verbose flag for per-binding undocumented names
       - Add Cmdliner argument for threshold override
     |))
   )
  )
  (subsection doc-formats
   (doc "Supported documentation comment formats")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       OCaml supports multiple doc comment styles. Borge recognizes:

       1. Standard docstring (ocamldoc style):
          (** Convert a string to a status value.
              @raise Invalid_argument if string is unknown *)
          let status_of_string s = ...

       2. Borg-style annotated comment:
          (* roerick note (|
             This function implements the status conversion logic.
             It handles all valid status strings defined in the spec.
           |) *)
          let status_of_string s = ...

       3. Borg-style annotated comment with literacy score:
          (* agent note [5250] (|
             The balance checker is a paranoid paren counter. We do
             NOT trust the parser for structural validation because
             a syntax error in the parser itself could hide a real
             bug. So balance is a separate, dumber implementation.

             This is defense in depth: the parser and balancer are
             independent. They should agree. When they don't, at
             least one is wrong.
           |) *)
          let check_balance input = ...

       4. Short borg comment:
          (*| Convert string to status. Raises on invalid input. |*)
          let status_of_string s = ...

       5. Exemption marker:
          (* exempt doc *)
          let internal_helper x = ...

       The literacy score [NNNN] is optional on borg-style comments.
       See the comment-writing-guide subsection for the full
       scoring system.

       All formats are extracted during drift --docs analysis and
       contribute to coverage calculations.
     |))
   )
  )
  (subsection drift-doc-integration
   (doc "How doc coverage is reported in drift and enforced")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       When borge drift --docs runs, findings are generated in two
       places:

       1. .borg.meta files get (documentation ...) blocks with full
          metrics. These are machine-readable and git-tracked.

       2. Drift findings list includes coverage-related findings:
          - (finding undocumented-module (file PATH) (coverage PERCENT))
          - (finding undocumented-function (file PATH) (name NAME))
            (only with --verbose flag)

       Thresholds and enforcement:
       - Coverage >= threshold: no finding (good)
       - Coverage 50%-threshold: warning-level finding
       - Coverage < 50%: error-level finding
       - Default threshold: 80% (0.8)

       Exit codes:
       - 0: all files meet threshold or above
       - 1: at least one file below 50% (error-level)
       - 2: at least one file between 50% and threshold (warning-level)
         only when --strict flag is passed; otherwise exit 0

       --strict flag:
       Without --strict, warnings are printed but do not cause a
       non-zero exit. With --strict, any file below the threshold
       (including warning-level) causes a non-zero exit. This allows
       CI to gradually enforce coverage: start without --strict,
       fix the errors, then add --strict to catch warnings too.

       The threshold is configurable per-project in borge.borg:
       (convention ocaml-dune
         (doc-coverage-threshold 0.7))

       Remaining work:
       - Wire Doc_coverage.meets_threshold into drift exit code logic
       - Add --strict and --verbose Cmdliner args to borge_drift.ml
       - Read doc-coverage-threshold from borge.borg convention stanza
     |))
   )
  )
  (subsection comment-writing-guide
   (doc "How to write code comments — literacy scoring for agents and humans")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       Comments are not checkboxes. They are how the codebase tells
       its story. The goal is not coverage — it is literacy. Can
       someone read this file like a book and understand it?

       Every borg-style comment carries a 4-digit literacy score:

         (* agent note [STORY EXPLAIN TEACH EDGE] (| ... |) *)

       Each digit is 0–7, self-declared by the author based on
       what the comment actually contains. Zeros are honest and
       common — most comments do not need to excel at everything.

       1. STORY — Does this comment connect to a narrative?
          0: No story. Just facts about this function.
          5: Has context. Explains why this exists in the story
             of the module — what came before, what comes after,
             why you're reading it now.
          7: Reads like a book chapter. You'd publish it. Someone
             could understand the module's architecture from the
             comments alone, without reading code.

       2. EXPLAIN — Does it explain what's happening in the code?
          0: Doesn't explain the implementation.
          5: Explains the algorithm or data flow. Someone could
             understand how the code works without reading every
             line.
          7: Walks you through it like a debugger session. Line
             by line where needed. Reveals the mechanics.

       3. TEACH — Does it teach something transferable?
          0: No transferable insight.
          5: Teaches a technique, pattern, or gotcha — something
             you'll use elsewhere in the codebase or in other
             projects.
          7: Teaches a fundamental concept. You'll remember it
             years later in unrelated code.

       4. EDGE — Does it say what breaks?
          0: No boundary discussion. Only the happy path.
          5: Major edge cases, preconditions, or failure modes
             mentioned. You know at least one thing that can go
             wrong.
          7: Every precondition stated, invariants explicit,
             failure modes catalogued. Reads like an incident
             report written by someone who got paged at 3am.

       Examples:

       A solid everyday comment — doesn't need to be a dissertation:
         (* agent note [5200] (|
            The balance checker is a paranoid paren counter. We
            do NOT trust the parser for structural validation
            because a parser bug could hide a real structural
            error. So balance is a separate, dumber implementation.

            This is defense in depth — two independent checks
            that should agree.
          |) *)

       A wrapper that needs almost no comment — honest zeros:
         (* agent note [5000] (|
            WRAPPER: balance-verbose is the same scanner as
            balance but with a paren stack for error reporting.
            Same algorithm, more memory.
          |) *)

       An edge-case-heavy function that needs thorough warning:
         (* agent note [5557] (|
            Extracts named elements from a UI element tree.

            Edge cases handled:
            - Empty tree: returns empty list (not an error)
            - Duplicate names: all occurrences collected, caller
              deduplicates if needed
            - Deep nesting: recurses through all children, O(depth)
            - Null names: elements without names are silently
              skipped (they're structural, not semantic)

            Invariant: returned list preserves document order.
            Do not sort it — downstream tools expect FIFO.
          |) *)

       Guidelines:
       - Score honestly. [5000] is not a failing grade — it means
         "I told the story but didn't explain mechanics, teach
         principles, or catalogue edge cases." That's fine for
         many functions.
       - Zeros are the default. Adding a dimension takes effort.
         If you didn't put edge cases in the comment, score EDGE
         as 0. Don't inflate scores to look good.
       - borge lint checks presence: every agent note must have a
         [NNNN] score block. Missing score is a lint warning.
       - borge lint checks plausibility: if you claim [7] on
         TEACH but the comment contains no transferable insight,
         lint suggests adjusting the score or adding content.
       - When a comment changes, reset the 4th digit (EDGE)
         and 1st digit (STORY if module context changed) to
         reflect unverified currency. Humans raise digits; agents
         should not claim currency they haven't verified.
       - Exempt internal helpers with (* exempt doc *) only if
         the name is truly self-documenting.
     |))
   )
  )
  (subsection code-doc-drift-status
   (doc "Mark inline code comments as drifted from implementation")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       When a code comment no longer accurately describes its
       function, it should be marked as drifted. This signals to
       agents and humans that the comment needs updating — it is
       not a silent lie, it is an explicit flag.

       Syntax: add (status drifted) to the borg-comment:
         (* agent note (|
            WHAT: Parse a .borg file into a typed AST.

            WHY: Downstream tools need structured access.
          |) (status drifted) *)
         let parse_file input = ...

       Or in the short borg format:
         (*| Parse .borg into typed AST. | (status drifted) *)

       Or in standard docstring:
         (** Parse a .borg file into a typed AST.
             @drifted comment does not mention error handling *)

       When to mark drifted:
       - An agent modifies a function's behavior but doesn't
         update the comment (agent should mark it, not ignore it)
       - A human changes code and notices the comment is stale
       - borge review detects doc-code mismatch and marks it

       When NOT to mark drifted:
       - Just update the comment instead. Drifted is for cases
         where you can't or won't fix it right now — you're
         acknowledging the gap, not endorsing it.

       Drift status in borg-comments:
       The (status drifted) form inside a borg-comment is
       analogous to (status drifted) in a .borg section. Both
       say: this description once matched reality, but no longer.

       Integration with borge review:
       - borge review checks for (status drifted) markers
       - If found, the finding is downgraded from "undocumented"
         to "drifted" — the function has a comment, but it's
         known-stale. This is better than undocumented (something
         was there) but worse than documented (it's accurate).
       - Coverage calculation: drifted comments count as
         documented for the numerator but are flagged separately
         in the report.

       Integration with borge lint:
       - (status drifted) markers produce a Stale_doc_comment
         lint warning (not error — the gap is acknowledged).
       - Without the marker, a stale comment that doesn't match
         the code is an unacknowledged lie — that should be
         caught by borge review (agentic), not lint (static).
     |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section code-comment-integrity
  (doc "Lint .ml files for missing documentation on exported bindings")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The existing comment-integrity section protects .borg file
        comments from silent agent deletion. This section extends
        lint to .ml code files, enforcing the literate-programming
        rule that every exported function must have a doc comment.

        This is the enforcement arm of code-documentation:
        drift --docs detects and reports coverage gaps; lint
        --code-doc makes it a pass/fail check.

        New lint issue types for .ml files:
        - Undocumented_binding: exported binding at PATH:LINE with
          name NAME has no preceding doc comment and no
          (* exempt doc *) marker. Severity: warning by default,
          error with --strict.
        - Stale_doc_comment: a doc comment exists but the binding
          signature below it has changed since the doc was last
          reviewed (future: compare against .borg.meta reviewed-at
          timestamp). Severity: warning.

        Integration with existing lint pipeline:
        - borge lint already walks .borg files. This adds a second
          pass over .ml files using Doc_extract.extract_bindings and
          Doc_detect.extract_file_docs.
        - New lint_issue variant:
          | Undocumented_binding of { path; name; line }
          | Stale_doc_comment of { path; name; line; last_reviewed }
        - These are included in lint_result.issues alongside existing
          .borg issues.
        - Error/warning classification follows drift-doc-integration
          thresholds: undocumented on exported binding = warning,
          undocumented with --strict = error.

        Exemptions:
        - Bindings starting with _ are internal, not checked.
        - (* exempt doc *) marker skips the binding.
        - Test bindings (let%) are skipped.
        - .mli-only modules: the .mli is the surface, but doc comments
          on .ml bindings are still expected. A binding with
          no .ml doc but a .mli doc is still flagged — the doc
          should live with the implementation.

        CLI:
        - borge lint (existing): now also checks .ml files when
          the code-comment-integrity section is present and not
          (status planned).
        - borge lint --code-doc: explicitly enable .ml file checks
          even if section status is planned (for gradual adoption).
        - No separate subcommand — this integrates into the existing
          lint flow.

        Exit code impact:
        - Undocumented_binding counts as warning in lint_result.
        - With --strict, counts as error.
        - lint already exits 1 if error_count > 0, 0 otherwise.

        Future:
        - Stale_doc_comment needs .borg.meta reviewed-at tracking.
        - Could integrate with semantic review: if borge review
          finds low doc-accuracy, that could promote the lint issue
          from warning to error.
      |))
  )
  (subsection lint-code-doc-flag
   (doc "borge lint --code-doc: enable .ml file doc checks")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       Adds a Cmdliner --code-doc flag to borge_lint.ml.

       When passed (or when code-comment-integrity status is not
       planned), the lint runner does a second pass:
       1. Find all .ml files via Doc_extract.find_ml_files
       2. For each file, extract bindings and docs
       3. For each exported binding without doc or exemption,
          emit Undocumented_binding lint issue
       4. Add to the existing lint_result and print alongside
          .borg issues

       Output format (consistent with existing lint):
         ⚠ lib/borge/spec.ml:42: undocumented binding 'parse_section'
         ⚠ lib/borge/drift.ml:15: undocumented binding 'check_code_drift'

       Or with --strict:
         ✗ lib/borge/spec.ml:42: undocumented binding 'parse_section'
     |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section ast-position-tracking
  (doc "Source position tracking on AST nodes for better diagnostics")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Currently only annotated comments have start_line/end_line. The
        sexp type itself carries no position info. Adding start_pos and
        end_pos to every sexp node would enable:
          - balance to report which ( at which line was closed early
          - nodes to show positions for each AST node
          - drift to map spec nodes to source locations
          - editors to jump to spec definitions
        The position type should include line, col, and offset to
        support both human-readable and machine-consumable output.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section make
  (doc "Agent-driven implementation — have an agent write code to match spec")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        borge make is the interactive entry point for agent-driven
        implementation. It opens a pi session with a prompt built from
        the spec, convention rules, existing code context, and the
        declared role. The agent writes code interactively; borge
        journals and commits after.

        borge make
          Open pi to implement planned sections. The agent reads the
          full spec, writes code and tests to match planned sections,
          and journals status changes. Auto-commits on clean exit.
          Calls borge balance first to ensure structural soundness.

        borge make spec "description"
          Single-shot spec generation. Not interactive. The agent
          produces a (status planned) section from a description.
          For interactive spec editing, use borge plan instead.

        borge drift --agent [--fix]
          Open pi to check alignment between spec and code. Read-only
          by default. --fix allows creating journal entries with
          suggestions for spec updates.

        borge commit
          Apply the current lock's journal to .borg files, run fmt
          and balance, git commit. Called automatically after
          borge make/plan unless --no-commit. Can be called
          manually after --no-commit sessions.

        All agent-driven commands create a .borge.lock/ directory
        (see lock-mechanism). The lock is removed after commit or
        abort.
      |))
  )
  (subsection make-command
   (doc "Make code match spec — implement planned sections")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge make: open pi with the implementer role. The agent
         reads the entire spec, identifies planned sections that are
         ready to implement, writes code and tests, adds journal
         entries. On clean exit: borge validates, commits.
         On error or --no-commit: lock stays, user reviews.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection plan-command
   (doc "Edit the spec — add sections, refine descriptions")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge plan: open pi with the spec-writer role. The agent
         reads the spec, identifies gaps, adds sections, refines
         descriptions, updates statuses. Journals all changes.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection drift-agent-command
   (doc "Check and optionally fix alignment between spec and code")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge drift --agent: open pi with the observer role. The
         agent reads spec and code, reports findings, marks drifted
         sections. No code changes. --fix adds the ability to
         write code repairs and tests. Neither mode may modify
         .borg files except section statuses.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection answer-command
   (doc "Answer ask comments in .borg files")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge answer: Scan .borg files for open ask comments and
         generate responses. An open ask is a (* author ask (|...|) *)
         comment without a corresponding (* agent response (|...|) *)
         or with an empty response slot.

         Usage:
           borge answer                    # Answer asks in current file
           borge answer --all              # Answer asks in all .borg files
           borge answer --file path.borg   # Answer asks in specific file
           borge answer --dry-run          # Show what would be answered

         Process:
         1. Parse specified .borg file(s) for ask comments
         2. Filter to asks without responses (or empty responses)
         3. For each open ask:
            - Build context from surrounding section
            - Query LLM with ask content + context
            - Insert (* agent response (|answer|) *) inline
         4. Journal all responses (role: analyst)

         Context building:
         - Parent section name and status
         - Sibling subsections (names only)
         - Preceding doc paragraphs (max 200 tokens)
         - The ask comment itself

         What is NOT included:
         - Full implementation code
         - Other .borg files (unless --all)
         - Historical responses or changelog

         Exit codes:
         - 0: All asks answered (or no asks found)
         - 1: LLM error or timeout
         - 2: Parse error in .borg file
         - 3: User abort (--no-commit mode)
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection context-management
   (doc "Minimal context to respect token limits")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The prompt sent to pi is intentionally minimal to avoid
         exhausting the agent's context window (~20K tokens consumed
         in seconds with full spec).

         Context structure by role:

         borge make (implementer)
           - Planned sections: name + one-line description (backlog)
           - Implemented sections: names only as guardrails
           - Existing module names: flat list
           - Max ~500 tokens total

         borge plan (spec-writer)
           - All section names with statuses
           - Section nesting structure
           - No full doc bodies in prompt (agent explores files)
           - Max ~800 tokens total

         borge drift --agent (observer/fixer)
           - Planned vs implemented diff
           - Changed module names since last commit
           - Optional: specific section under investigation
           - Max ~600 tokens total

         What is NOT loaded:
         - Full .borg file contents (only summaries)
         - Module export lists (just names)
         - Convention rule details (just convention name)
         - Historical commits or diffs

         The agent uses `read` tool to explore files on demand
         rather than having everything pre-loaded.
       |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section execute
  (doc "Make code match spec — interactive agent mode with role-gated behavior")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Agent-driven commands open pi interactively with a minimal
        prompt built from summarized spec data. The agent's job depends
        on mode: implementer, spec-writer, or observer. Context is
        intentionally limited (see context-management subsection).

        The agent operates in a .borge.lock/ workspace (see lock-
        mechanism). It edits .borg files in .borge.lock/working/ and
        code files in the repo. Commit applies working/ back to repo
        after integrity and balance checks.

        Commands:

        borge make
          Role: implementer
          CAN: write code, write tests, add journal entries (status
               changes, notes)
          CANNOT: modify .borg files (reads from working/, like all
                  agents, but its writes go to code files and journal)
          CANNOT: delete human-authored comments
          INTENT: make code match the spec — planned sections are
                  the backlog, implemented sections are guardrails

        borge plan
          Role: spec-writer
          CAN: modify .borg files in .borge.lock/working/
          CANNOT: write code files
          CANNOT: delete human-authored comments (integrity check
                  blocks commit if violated --force to override)
          INTENT: edit the spec — add sections, refine descriptions,
                  update priorities, reorder

        borge drift --agent [--fix]
          Role: observer (default) or fixer (--fix)
          CAN (observer): create findings as journal entries, add
                          comments to spec, change section status to
                          drifted
          CANNOT (observer): write code files, modify .borg except
                             via journal/status
          CAN (fixer): all observer permissions + write code repairs
                       and tests
          CANNOT (fixer): modify .borg files except via journal/status
          INTENT: check alignment. --fix repairs drift.

        borge commit
          Apply .borge.lock/working/ back to repo, run fmt and
          balance, git commit. Called automatically after make/plan
          unless --no-commit. Blocked by human comment deletion
          (unless --force).

        borge abort
          Discard .borge.lock/. .borg files in repo are untouched.
          Code files may have been modified — use git to undo.

        Why auto-commit? Git is the audit trail. Each session produces
        a commit. Revert if wrong. Review at PR level, not per-change.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section inbox
  (doc "Unified triage queue for scope changes, bugs, TODOs, and escalation reports")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The inbox absorbs the old issue-tracker section. Everything
        that needs human attention before it becomes a spec change
        goes through the inbox — bugs, drift findings, scope
        concerns, TODOs, external reports. One queue, one format,
        one lifecycle.

        Why the inbox replaced the old bug tracker:
        The old .borge-bug format and the inbox concept were the
        same shape — both are "something that needs review before
        it affects the spec." Keeping them separate meant two file
        formats, two lifecycles, two sets of commands. A bug
        report from a chatbot and a scope concern from an agent
        are both inbox items that need triage.

        The inbox also enforces spec-first discipline at the tool
        level — not just by convention. The current borge discipline
        says "no unspec'd code," but nothing prevents an agent from
        quietly editing a .borg file during implementation. The
        inbox fixes this:

        - Agents in Plan mode CAN edit .borg files directly
        - Agents in any other mode CANNOT edit .borg files
        - Instead they file inbox items requesting spec changes
        - Inbox items require human review before becoming spec
        - This prevents unseen scope drift

        Five sources of inbox items:

        1. AGENT-FILED SCOPE CONCERNS. During implementation,
           if an agent discovers the spec is wrong or incomplete,
           it does NOT edit the spec. It files an inbox item
           describing the needed change and why. This forces the
           scope change to be visible and reviewed. (This replaces
           the old architectural-decisions subsection — same
           concept, now part of a unified queue.)

        2. BUG REPORTS. From agents, users, or chatbots. A bug
           is an inbox item where something is broken. Drift
           findings from borge drift automatically create bug-type
           inbox items. Semantic review findings (from borge review)
           also create bug-type items.

        3. TODO AGGREGATION. All TODO markers across .borg files
           and .ml files are surfaced as inbox items. A TODO in
           code is an implicit spec gap — it says "I didn't
           implement what was planned" or "something was planned
           but not yet spec'd." Either way it needs triage.

        4. EXTERNAL FILINGS. Users and chatbots can file inbox
           items directly — bug reports, scope concerns, feature
           requests, "this was implemented differently than I
           expected." These are the same shape as agent-filed
           items but come from outside the pipeline.

        5. DRIFT AND REVIEW FINDINGS. borge drift and borge review
           produce findings automatically. These are linked to
           inbox items instead of being a parallel tracking system.

        Inbox item types (source field):
        - scope-concern: spec is wrong, incomplete, or ambiguous
        - bug-report: something is broken
        - todo-aggregation: surfaced TODO marker
        - feature-request: user or chatbot wants something new
        - implementation-divergence: code doesn't match spec
        - drift-finding: from borge drift --agent
        - review-finding: from borge review (semantic or doc)

        Lifecycle:
        triage → acknowledged → plan-approved → spec-updated → closed

        The critical transition is plan-approved → spec-updated.
        Only Plan mode can make it. Only after human review.
        This is what prevents scope drift.

        Other resolutions:
        - wontfix: acknowledged but not acting on it
        - duplicate: same as an existing item
        - fixed-in-code: bug fixed without spec change
      |))
  )
  (subsection inbox-file-format
   (doc "File format for inbox items")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Inbox items are stored as .borge-inbox files in the repo,
         tracked in git alongside code and specs. One queue, one
         format.

         Scope concern example:
         (borge-inbox id:"INBOX-7"
           (title "Need error handling in db_sql")
           (status triage)
           (filed-by agent:implementer)
           (source scope-concern)
           (affects section:"lib.borg:db-sql")
           (created 2026-05-31)
           (doc (|
             During implementation of db_sql.ml, discovered that
             the spec doesn't cover connection retry logic.
             The spec says (status implemented) for db-sql but
             retry was never mentioned. Filing for scope review.
           |))
         )

         Bug report example:
         (borge-inbox id:"INBOX-12"
           (title "parse_expr crashes on nested parens")
           (status triage)
           (filed-by roerick)
           (source bug-report)
           (affects section:"lib.borg:parser")
           (created 2026-05-31)
           (doc (|
             borge parse crashes on the new borge.borg with
             nested verbatim strings. Repro: borge parse borge.borg.
           |))
         )

         Drift-finding example:
         (borge-inbox id:"INBOX-15"
           (title "Doc-code drift in lock_mechanism.ml")
           (status triage)
           (filed-by agent:drift)
           (source drift-finding)
           (affects section:"lib.borg:lock")
           (created 2026-05-31)
           (drift-report-id "DRIFT-2026-05-31-001")
           (doc (|
             Comment says "copies original/ back to the repo"
             but code actually copies from .borge.lock/snapshot/.
           |))
         )

         Fields:
         - id: unique identifier (INBOX-N)
         - title: brief description
         - status: triage | acknowledged | plan-approved | spec-updated | closed
         - filed-by: who filed it (roerick, agent:implementer, agent:drift, chatbot:name)
         - source: scope-concern | bug-report | todo-aggregation | feature-request |
                   implementation-divergence | drift-finding | review-finding
         - affects: which .borg section is impacted
         - created: date
         - doc: full explanation
         - relates-to: optional reference to another INBOX-N
         - drift-report-id: optional link to drift report that spawned this item
         - resolution: optional (when date) (how text) (by who)
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection inbox-commands
   (doc "CLI commands for inbox management")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge inbox list [--status triage] [--source bug-report]
           List inbox items, optionally filtered by status and source.

         borge inbox show INBOX-7
           Show full details of an inbox item.

         borge inbox file --title "description" --source scope-concern
           File a new inbox item. Used by agents, users, and chatbots.
           The --source flag determines the type. The --affects flag
           optionally links to a .borg section.

         borge inbox acknowledge INBOX-7
           Mark as acknowledged by human. Moves to acknowledged.

         borge inbox approve INBOX-7
           Approve for spec update. Moves to plan-approved.
           This DOES NOT update the spec yet — it just grants
           permission for Plan mode to incorporate it.

         borge inbox close INBOX-7 [--resolution spec-updated|wontfix|duplicate|fixed-in-code]
           Close an inbox item with resolution.

         borge inbox consolidate
           Aggregate all TODO markers across .borg and .ml
           files into inbox items. Deduplicates against
           existing items. Also surfaces (status planned)
           sections with no details.

         The old commands are subsumed:
         - "borge bug new" → "borge inbox file --source bug-report"
         - "borge bug list" → "borge inbox list --source bug-report"
         - "borge bug show ID" → "borge inbox show ID"
         - "borge bug close ID" → "borge inbox close ID"
         - "borge drift-report" → drift auto-creates inbox items
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection inbox-gating-rules
   (doc "How borge modes enforce inbox usage")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The inbox is enforced through mode-specific gating.
         This is hard enforcement at the tool level, not convention.

         In Plan mode:
         - CAN edit .borg files (including plan sections)
         - CAN close inbox items with spec-updated
         - This is the ONLY mode that can modify plan content

         In Execute mode:
         - CANNOT edit .borg files (plan sections are frozen)
         - MUST file inbox items for any spec concerns
         - Can edit code files freely
         - CANNOT edit human-authored comments in any file
           (see comment-protection subsection)

         In Audit/Review mode:
         - CANNOT edit .borg files
         - CAN file inbox items for drift/review findings
         - Drift and review findings auto-create inbox items
         - CANNOT edit human-authored comments in any file

         In BorgEdit mode:
         - CAN edit .borg files (explicit escalation)
         - But changes are tracked and logged
         - Should still file inbox items for major changes
         - CANNOT edit human-authored comments in any file

         The enforcement is at the tool level:
         rein's gate engine rejects edit/write tools targeting
         .borg files when not in Plan or BorgEdit mode.
         rein's gate engine also rejects edits to human-authored
         comments in .ml files (see comment-protection).
         Agents must use the inbox file command instead.
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection comment-protection
   (doc "Protect human-authored comments in both .borg and .ml files")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The lock-mechanism already protects human-authored comments
         in .borg files (detecting deletions via diff at commit time).
         This extends the same principle to .ml files.

         What counts as a human-authored comment:
         In .borg files:
         - (* roerick note ... *)
         - (* <name> ask ... *)

         In .ml files:
         - (** ... *) ocamldoc comments (not authored by agents)
         - (* roerick ... *) or (* <human-name> ... *)
         - (*| ... |*) borge-style comments with human authorship

         What does NOT count as human-authored:
         - (* agent note (|...|) *)
         - (* exempt doc *)
         - (* TODO: ... *)  (these get aggregated into inbox)
         - Regular code comments without authorship markers

         Enforcement:
         At the tool level, rein's gate engine inspects edit
         operations on both .borg and .ml files. If the edit
         would modify or delete a human-authored comment, the
         gate BLOCKS it — regardless of mode. The only exception
         is when the human explicitly approves the edit (ASK).

         This is stricter than the .borg-only protection in the
         lock-mechanism. The lock checks at commit time after
         the session. The gate checks BEFORE the edit happens.

         Why: an agent should not be able to silently change what
         a human said in a comment. If the comment is wrong, file
         an inbox item with source scope-concern explaining the
         discrepancy. A human reviews it. The human can then run
         borge normalize to accept the change intentionally.
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection semantic-review
   (doc "LLM-powered verification that function docs match implementation")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Walks OCaml files, extracts functions and their docstrings,
         uses LLM to verify two things:

         1. Doc-code alignment: Does the comment accurately describe
            what the function does and why it exists?
         2. Internal consistency: Is the function well-constructed,
            or does it have internal contradictions (dead code paths,
            unreachable branches, parameters that are always ignored,
            logic that contradicts the comment)?

         Both checks happen in a single LLM pass per function for
         efficiency. The prompt asks the LLM to evaluate both.

         Process per function:
         1. Extract: function name, signature, docstring, body
         2. Prompt LLM with three questions:
            a) Is there a docstring present? If yes, does it
               accurately describe WHAT the function does and
               WHY it exists? (doc-code alignment)
            b) Are there (status drifted) markers in the
               comment? If so, the drift is acknowledged — flag
               as drifted rather than inaccurate.
            c) Is the function internally consistent? Are there
               dead branches, unused parameters, contradictory
               logic, or structural issues? (internal consistency)
         3. Store result in .borg.meta under node metadata

         What counts as doc-code drift:
         - Comment says "returns Error on invalid input" but the
           function actually raises an exception
         - Comment describes behavior that no longer exists
         - Comment omits important side effects or error cases
         - Comment says WHY but the reason has changed (e.g.,
           "Workaround for bug X" but bug X was fixed)

         What counts as internal inconsistency:
         - Parameter is accepted but never used in the body
         - Match branch is unreachable (covered by earlier branch)
         - Boolean parameter that is always passed the same value
         - Let binding that shadows an outer binding identically
         - Comment says one thing, code does another (this is also
           doc-code drift, but indicates the function itself may
           have been patched without thinking through the design)

         Metadata structure:
         (function name:"parse_expr"
           (doc-present true)
           (doc-status accurate|drifted|missing)
           (doc-accuracy high|medium|low)
           (consistency consistent|questionable|inconsistent)
           (internal-issues "optional description")
           (confidence high|medium|low)
           (checked-at 2025-05-16T10:30:00Z)
         )

         doc-status values:
         - accurate: comment matches implementation
         - drifted: comment has (status drifted) marker OR
           LLM detected significant mismatch
         - missing: no doc comment present

         consistency values:
         - consistent: no internal issues detected
         - questionable: minor issues (unused parameter, etc.)
         - inconsistent: major issues (dead branches, logic
           contradictions, code that can never execute)

         Commands:
         - borge review FILE
         - borge review --dir DIR
         - borge review --all
         - borge review --stale
         - borge review --focus drift
         - borge review --focus consistency

         Findings flow to inbox:
         - Low doc-accuracy → creates inbox item (source: review-finding)
         - Missing doc on exported function → inbox item
         - (status drifted) comment → informational inbox item
         - Internal inconsistency → inbox item (source: review-finding)

         Auto-marking drifted comments:
         When borge review --all detects doc-code drift on a
         function that does NOT have (status drifted), it can
         optionally add the marker:
           borge review --all --mark-drifted
         This inserts (status drifted) into the comment. The
         marker means "a machine checked this and found drift"
         — it is not a fix, but it makes the lie explicit.
         Without --mark-drifted, review only reports findings.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection distributed-specs
   (doc "Module-level .borg files with loose function-level coupling")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Rather than one central borge.borg, each module folder has
         its own .borg file describing that module's exports and
         structure. Function-level detail lives in borg-comments
         within the .ml files themselves.

         This creates a hierarchy:
         - repo/borge.borg: conventions, top-level goals
         - lib/core/lib_core.borg: module exports, dependencies
         - lib/core/spec.ml: functions with borg-comments

         Borg-comment format (OCaml comment containing borg):
         (* (fn extract_functions
              (doc "Pulls functions from code")
              (since "2025-05")) *)

         Drift checking (static/non-agentic):
         Only verifies referential integrity — does a function name
         listed in the module's exports actually exist in the code?
         Does a function in the code appear in the exports?
         No semantic comparison between module spec and function docs.

         Drift checking (agentic):
         The agent reads module spec intent and function comments,
         determines if there's semantic drift, and can file inbox
         items for mismatches it finds.

         This keeps the non-agentic path fast and simple while
         delegating semantic understanding to the LLM when needed.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection todo-aggregation
   (doc "Surfacing TODOs as inbox items")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         TODOs are spec gaps. They say "I know I need to do
         something here but I haven't spec'd it yet." The inbox
         consolidates them.

         TODO sources:
         - TODO in .borg files:
           (* TODO: handle connection retry *)
         - TODO in .ml files:
           (* TODO: add error handling *)
         - (status planned) sections with no details in .borg
         - Drift findings from borge drift

         borge inbox consolidate scans all sources and creates
         or updates inbox items. Existing items are NOT
         duplicated — the consolidation checks for matches.

         A TODO in code during implementation is NOT a license
         to implement without spec. It's a signal that the
         spec is incomplete. File it, triage it, plan it.
      |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section lock-mechanism
  (doc "Transactional isolation for agent sessions — metadata, not workspace")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Every agent-driven command (make, plan, drift --agent) creates
        a .borge.lock/ directory. The lock is a transaction log and
        metadata store, NOT a workspace. The agent edits .borg files
        directly in the repo. The lock captures the session for audit,
        integrity checking, and abort/restore.

        The agent's workspace IS the repo. The lock provides:

        1. Concurrency prevention. If .borge.lock/ exists, refuse new
           agent sessions. One agent at a time prevents conflicting
           edits. The human can `borge abort` to clear a stale lock.

        2. Snapshot for integrity check. On lock creation, all .borg
           files are copied to .borge.lock/original/. At commit time,
           borge diffs original/ against the working tree to detect
           deletions of human-authored comments. If found, commit is
           blocked unless --force.

        3. Snapshot for abort. If the session goes wrong, `borge abort`
           copies original/ back to the repo, restoring .borg files.
           Code file changes are NOT reverted — git handles that.

        4. Audit trail. The lock contains:
           - prompt.md: the full prompt sent to pi
           - journal: structured log of proposed changes
           - role: the role declaration for the session
           - state: running | completed | failed

        5. Commit message construction. The journal provides the
           summary for the auto-generated git commit message. Each
           entry includes the reason for the change.

        Lock directory layout:
          .borge.lock/
            original/            # snapshot of all .borg files at lock time
            role                 # role declaration (text + sexp)
            prompt.md            # prompt sent to pi
            journal              # structured session log
            state                # running | completed | failed

        Agent editing rules:
        - .borg files: edit in place (repo root). The agent uses normal
          paths like lib.borg, borge.borg, etc.
        - Code files: edit in place (repo tree). Standard paths.
        - At commit: diff checks, fmt, balance, git commit.
        - At abort: restore .borg files from original/. Code files stay
          as-is; use git to undo if needed.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section review
  (doc "Review code quality against spec using LLM")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Every file should carry a quality review — this is the review
        section in each .borg child spec. borge review generates a
        code-quality assessment for files under each spec section.
        The review answers: Does the code match the spec description?
        Are exports documented? Are there dead functions? Is error
        handling present? The review is stored as annotated comments
        in the spec or as a separate .review file.
      |))
  )
  (subsection review-command
   (doc "Generate a per-file code quality review from spec")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge review FILE: read the spec for the file, read the
         file source, build prompt asking pi to assess:
         - Does every spec described feature exist in code?
         - Are all exports documented? (check .mli, or docstrings)
         - Are there dead functions (unused exports)?
         - Is error handling missing where the spec says 'returns error'?
         - Is the literate description accurate?
         Output: findings as sexp, similar to drift findings.
         These are stored as annotated comments under the file's
         spec section, or in a per-file .review.borg file.
       |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section literate-programming
  (doc "The borge philosophy: spec is the primary artifact, code is derived")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        In a borge project, the spec is not documentation — it IS the
        source of truth. The .borg file is the artifact. Borge reads
        specs, validates them, reports on them, and helps agents edit
        them. But borge does NOT generate code. Every .borg file is
        both a human-readable design document and a machine-checkable
        contract.

        The three pillars:

        1. Spec first: write (status planned) sections before code.
           Use borge make spec to turn todos into structured specs.

        2. Implement via agent: borge make opens an interactive session
           where an agent implements planned sections. The agent writes
           code AND updates the spec, journaling changes. This is NOT
           code generation — the spec is authored, not derived.

        3. Verify continuously: borge balance, check, drift, and review
           keep spec and code aligned. Drift means the implementation
           has diverged from the spec. Fix the code or update the spec.

        A completed project is one where every section is (status
        implemented), every file has a code review, and borge drift
        reports zero items.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section agent-discipline
  (doc "Rules for AI agents working in borge — operationalizing spec-first development")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The literate-programming section declares the philosophy. This
        section declares the operational rules that follow from it.
        These rules apply to AI agents (like pi) and humans alike,
        but AI agents are the primary audience because they are the
        ones most likely to violate them through scope creep.

        Rule 1: No unspec'd code. Every .ml file, every module, every
        significant function must have a corresponding .borg section
        before it is written. If you want to add db_sql.ml, first add
        a (subsection db-sql) to lib.borg with (status planned) and a
        doc block describing what it does. Then implement. This is not
        bureaucracy — it is how borge prevents drift. Code without a
        spec is code without an owner.

        Rule 2: Read the spec before building. Before implementing
        any feature, read the relevant .borg sections — especially
        their (details ...) blocks and (status ...). A (status planned)
        section with detailed doc is a design document: follow it. A
        section marked (status planned) with no details is a blank
        check: ask the human before filling it in. A feature not in
        any .borg file is out of scope — do not build it without
        explicit instruction.

        Rule 3: Follow existing patterns. When the codebase has an
        established pattern (like generate.ml using Agent.run_pi_print
        for LLM-mediated generation), and the spec describes the same
        pattern (like ui.borg's generation-workflow section saying
        "assemble prompt, call the LLM"), follow that pattern. Do not
        invent alternative approaches (like template rendering) unless
        the spec explicitly calls for them.

        Rule 4: Update spec status after implementation. When you
        implement something that was (status planned) in a .borg file,
        update it to (status implemented). When you add a new module,
        add a corresponding subsection. Stale statuses are a form of
        drift — they make borge report inaccurate and mislead the
        next agent about what is done vs. what is pending.

        Rule 5: Check against spec during loops. In iterative
        workflows (Ralph loops, multi-step tasks), periodically
        verify: does my task list match what the .borg files say?
        Am I building something the spec describes, or am I building
        something I invented? If a task item has no corresponding
        .borg section, stop and ask before proceeding.

        Rule 6: Agents write only agent comments. An agent may only
        create, modify, or delete comments where the authorship is
        "agent" or "bot". Agents may NOT create, modify, or delete
        comments attributed to humans ("roerick" or any non-agent
        author). If an agent needs to quote a human, it uses
        (* agent note (|The user said: ...|) *). If a human wants a
        conversation captured in the spec, the human writes
        (* <name> ask ...) and the agent writes (* agent response ...).
        This rule is enforced by the lock integrity check (detects
        inserted and deleted human comments) and by lint (detects
        added human comments via git diff).
      |))
  )
 )
 (section spec-language-design
  (doc "Design principles for the borge spec language — declaration without evaluation")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The .borg format is a declaration language, not a programming
        language. A .borg file states what is true and what should be
        true. The tools verify whether reality matches the statement.
        The spec language does not evaluate, branch, loop, or mutate.
        It declares.

        This boundary — declaration without evaluation — is the
        defining architectural choice. Keeping it firm gives borge
        its most important properties:

        - Forward compatibility: unknown forms survive parsing
        - DSL extensibility: UI, DB, inbox coexist without parser changes
        - Human readability: the format stays small and learnable
        - Self-description: the grammar lives in borge.borg, not in types

        The risk of over-typing the core is that every new keyword
        form becomes a compiler change. Borge avoids this. The core
        Ast.sexp type stays generic. Typed consumption happens in
        consumer modules (spec.ml, ui_parse.ml, db_parse.ml) that
        interpret the generic tree.

        Where stronger types genuinely help is at the EXTRACTION
        boundary — the code that turns raw sexp into typed records.
        The current pattern is hand-rolled pattern matching repeated
        in every consumer. This is brittle: no exhaustiveness, silent
        skips, repeated logic.

        GADTs for extraction combinators (lib/core/extract.ml):
        A small combinator library with a GADT encoding "this extractor
        produces type 'a from a sexp". Example:

          val atom : string t
          val string : string t
          val keyword : string -> 'a t -> 'a t
          val ( let* ) : 'a t -> ('a -> 'b t) -> 'b t

        Then status extraction becomes:

          let status_extractor =
            let* st = keyword "status" atom in
            match status_of_string st with
            | Some s -> return s
            | None -> fail (`Invalid_status st)

        This stays entirely in OCaml's type system. The .borg format
        does not change. UI and DB DSLs define their own extractors
        on top of this core, replacing ~200 lines of hand-rolled
        pattern matching in ui_parse.ml and db_parse.ml with
        composable, testable declarations.

        Phantom types for the tool pipeline:
        Borge has a conceptual pipeline: balance -> parse -> lint
        -> drift -> review. A lightweight phantom type enforces this
        order:

          type balanced  type parsed  type linted
          type 'stage file = { ast : Ast.file; stage : 'stage }
          val balance : string -> balanced file result
          val parse : balanced file -> parsed file result
          val lint : parsed file -> linted file result

        Now lint cannot be called on a raw string. This is about
        the tools, not the spec language. It prevents real bugs
        with zero runtime cost.

        Richer constraint forms (without evaluation):
        The spec language can express more expectations without
        becoming executable. Declared constraints are verified by
        tools, not evaluated by the language:

          (depends-on STRING)
            -> resolved by borge future to build roadmaps
          (requires FORM)
            -> checked by borge lint
          (asserts FORM)
            -> verified by borge drift

        These are declarations of intent. The language does not
        resolve, check, or verify them. The tools do. This keeps
        .borg as a vocabulary for expectations while the typechecker
        of reality remains outside the language.

        Effects are not appropriate for borge:
        The current architecture is linear: read -> parse -> analyze
        -> produce findings. Effects shine with suspensible
        computations, plugin architectures, or complex resource
        management. Borge has none of these at scale. Effects
        would introduce non-local control flow into a simple
        linear pipeline, making it harder to follow.

        The one future place effects might fit is the LLM call
        layer (generate.ml, review_agent.ml). A perform (LLM_query
        prompt) effect with a configurable handler could cleanly
        separate "request evaluation" from "how that request is
        fulfilled" (real model, cache, timeout, mock). But even
        there, a functor-based interface is sufficient today.

        Self-describing validation:
        Since .borg files describe the .borg format in borge.borg
        itself, the tools could extract a schema from borge.borg
        and validate arbitrary .borg files against it. The spec
        describes the spec language. The tools validate conformity.
        This is the ultimate "types without evaluation": the
        language has no typechecker, but the tooling uses the
        self-description as a schema.
      |))
  )
  (subsection extract-combinators
   (doc "GADT-based extraction library for typed sexp consumers")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         lib/core/extract.ml: a GADT combinator library that replaces
         hand-rolled pattern matching in spec.ml, ui_parse.ml, and
         db_parse.ml.

         Types:
         - 'a t : an extractor that produces 'a from a sexp

         Primitives:
         - atom : string t
         - string : string t
         - int : int t
         - keyword : string -> 'a t -> 'a t
         - list : 'a t -> 'a list t
         - optional : 'a t -> 'a option t
         - record : fields -> 'a t

         Combinators:
         - ( let* ) : sequential composition
         - return : lift pure value into extractor
         - fail : raise structured error with context

         Usage in spec.ml:
         Replace the hand-rolled extract functions with combinator
         pipelines. The compiler checks exhaustiveness and type
         safety.

         Migration plan:
         1. Implement extract.ml with primitives and combinators
         2. Port spec.ml to use combinators (smallest surface)
         3. Verify edge case parity with existing tests
         4. Port ui_parse.ml and db_parse.ml
         5. Remove duplicated extraction helpers from each module
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection pipeline-phantoms
   (doc "Phantom types enforcing tool pipeline order at compile time")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Add phantom types to the file processing pipeline to
         prevent tools from being called on data at the wrong
         stage.

         Types:
         type balanced  type parsed  type linted  type checked
         type 'stage file = { ast : Ast.file; stage : 'stage }

         Functions (typed interface):
         Balance.balance : string -> balanced file result
         Parse.parse_file : string -> parsed file result
         Parse.from_balanced : balanced file -> parsed file result
         Lint.run : parsed file -> linted file result
         Check.run : linted file -> checked file result

         This is zero-cost: the 'stage type parameter is erased
         at runtime. The typechecker prevents bugs like calling
         lint on a raw filename string or on a file that bypassed
         balance. It documents the intended pipeline in types.
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection richer-constraints
   (doc "Declares more expectations in .borg without making it executable")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Extend the language vocabulary with forms that declare
         expectations. These are not evaluated by the language;
         they are verified by the tools.

         (depends-on STRING)
           Declares that this section requires another section to
           be implemented first. Used by borge future to build
           dependency-aware roadmaps and detect ordering mistakes.

         (requires FORM)
           Declares invariants this section depends on.
           Examples: (requires (min-coverage 0.8))
                     (requires (max-complexity 12))
           Checked by borge lint. Violations produce findings.

         (asserts FORM)
           Declares expectations about code reality.
           Examples: (asserts (module-surface "drift.ml" exports 12))
                     (asserts (no-orphan-files))
           Verified by borge drift. Mismatches are drift findings.

         All three forms are parseable by the generic sexp parser.
         Spec.ml recognizes them as first-class metadata. The
         tools decide whether and how to enforce them.
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection effects-policy
   (doc "Why effects are deferred — and where they might fit later")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Effects are deferred because borge's architecture does not
         need them. The tool pipeline is linear and deterministic.
         Adding effects would make control flow implicit and harder
         to trace.

         Current position: do not use effects in borge.

         Future consideration: the LLM abstraction layer
         (generate.ml, review_agent.ml) may benefit from an effect
         that separates "request evaluation" from "how it is
         fulfilled" when testing and mocking become a burden.
         Until then, functor-based interfaces are sufficient.
      |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section config
  (doc "Project-level and user-level configuration for borge")
  (status planned)
  (subsection config-file
   (doc ".borgerc: per-project configuration in sexp format")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
          Borge reads configuration from .borgerc files. Search order:
            1. Project root: .borgerc (checked into git, shared by team)
            2. User global: ~/.config/borge/config (not in git, personal)
          Project config overrides user config. If both define the same
          key, the project value wins.

          The file uses the same sexp format as .borg files.
          An empty or missing .borgerc means all defaults apply.

          Config file format:
          (borge-config
            (developer-name "roerick")
            (api-keys
              (anthropic (env ANTHROPIC_API_KEY))
              (openai (env OPENAI_API_KEY)))
          )

          Keys:
          - (developer-name STRING): the author name used for annotated
            comments. When set, borge make/plan/drift create comments
            with this authorship instead of "agent". When unset,
            defaults to "agent". This is the name that appears in
            (* developer-name note (|...|) *) comments.

          - (api-keys ...): map of provider names to API key sources.
            Each key is either:
              (PROVIDER (env ENV_VAR_NAME)) — read from environment
              (PROVIDER (value "literal-key")) — literal string (NOT
                recommended for checked-in files; prefer env refs)
            When borge needs to call an LLM directly (not via pi),
            it reads the relevant provider key from config. If not
            configured, borge falls back to pi or BORGE_AGENT_CMD
            as it does today.

          Why sexp format: consistency with the rest of borge.
          The parser already exists. No new syntax to learn.
          No TOML/YAML dependency. The config is a borge document.

          Why not just env vars: env vars work but don't carry
          developer-name or project-scoped settings. The config file
          gives a named, version-controlled, discoverable home for
          project preferences that env vars can't provide.

          Loading: lib/config.ml reads .borgerc, falls back to
          ~/.config/borge/config, merges project over user.
          The result is a typed config record available to all
          borge commands.
        |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section merge-queue
  (doc "CI/CD pipeline for agentic development — isolated worktrees, batched merges")
  (status planned)
  (subsection agent-isolation
   (doc "Every agent run is isolated in its own worktree and branch")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM: Agents running concurrently clobber each other's
         changes, modify files directly in main worktree, create
         chaos that humans must clean up.

         SOLUTION: Pi harness enforces isolation at the tool level.
         Every `pi --mode agent` invocation:

         1. Creates unique branch: agent/run-{timestamp}-{uuid}
         2. Creates worktree: .pi/worktrees/agent-{id}/
         3. Runs entirely within that worktree
         4. On completion: submits to borge merge-queue
         5. On failure: preserves worktree for debugging

         HARNESS RULES (enforced by pi, not optional):
         - If .pi/concurrent.lock shows target files already locked,
           queue behind existing agent or refuse to start
         - If worktree creation fails, agent cannot proceed
         - If branch already exists, agent cannot proceed
         - Agent never modifies files outside its worktree

         LOCKFILE FORMAT (.pi/concurrent.lock):
         {
           "active_runs": [
             {"id": "agent-123", "branch": "agent/run-...", 
              "files": ["lib/parser.ml"], "since": "2026-05-17T..."}
           ]
         }

         This prevents conflicts BEFORE they happen, not after.
         |))
   )
   (depends-on
    "pi harness worktree support"
   )
  )
  (subsection merge-queue
   (doc "Central queue of pending agent changes, processed atomically")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         After agent completes in isolated worktree, it submits to
         the merge queue rather than applying directly:

         borge merge-queue submit \
           --from-worktree .pi/worktrees/agent-123/ \
           --summary "Generated parser section" \
           --confidence 0.92 \
           --affected-files lib/parser.ml,test/parser.ml

         QUEUE STATE:
         - PENDING: Submitted, waiting for merge
         - READY: No conflicts with queued items before it
         - BLOCKED: Conflicts with earlier queue item
         - MERGING: Currently being processed
         - MERGED: Successfully merged to main
         - REJECTED: Merge failed, worktree preserved

         CONFLICT DETECTION:
         borge analyzes file overlap between queue items:
         agent/run-123 touches lib/parser.ml
         agent/run-124 touches lib/parser.ml  → BLOCKED behind 123
         agent/run-125 touches lib/lexer.ml   → READY (no overlap)

         Commands:
         borge merge-queue list        # Show pending items
         borge merge-queue show 123    # Diff for specific item
         borge merge --next            # Merge first ready item
         borge merge --all             # Merge all ready items
         borge merge --dry-run         # Show what would happen

         AUTO-MERGE AGENT (optional):
         - Daemon that continuously runs `borge merge --all`
         - Configurable policies: batch size, delay between merges
         - Can pause on failure, wait for human review
         |))
   )
   (depends-on
    "git worktree management"
    "conflict detection"
   )
  )
  (subsection merge-strategies
   (doc "How to integrate agent changes into main")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         MERGE STRATEGIES (configurable per project):

         1. FAST-FORWARD (default for linear history):
            - If main hasn't moved since agent branched: git merge --ff
            - Cleanest history, no merge commits
            - Fails if main has new commits (use rebase instead)

         2. REBASE-THEN-MERGE:
            - Rebase agent branch onto latest main
            - Then fast-forward merge
            - Linear history even when main moves
            - Risk: conflicts during rebase need agent re-run

         3. MERGE-COMMIT:
            - Always create merge commit
            - Preserves agent branch history
            - Easier to revert entire agent run if needed

         4. SQUASH:
            - Squash all agent commits to single commit
            - Clean history, lose agent's internal iterations
            - Good for: simple changes, auto-accepted runs

         ROLLBACK ON FAILURE:
         If merge fails or CI rejects:
         1. Revert merge commit (if created)
         2. Mark queue item REJECTED
         3. Preserve worktree for debugging
         4. Notify agent owner (human or meta-agent)
         5. Block dependent queue items

         ATOMICITY:
         Either entire agent run merges or none of it does.
         No partial merges leaving codebase broken.
         |))
   )
   (depends-on
    "merge queue"
    "git strategies"
   )
  )
  (subsection victor-integration
   (doc "IDE visibility into merge pipeline, not control")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Victor's role is OBSERVABILITY, not orchestration.
         Borge owns the queue; Victor just shows it.

         UI ELEMENTS:
         - Status bar: "🔄 3 pending merges" or "✅ Queue clear"
         - Heatmap: Files with pending agent changes glow differently
         - Popup on agent complete: "Submitted to merge queue #123"
         - Diff preview: Click queue item → see what agent changed
         - Emergency brake: "PAUSE ALL MERGES" button (sets borge flag)

         NO APPLY/DISCARD BUTTONS:
         - Agents don't ask "should I apply?"
         - They submit to queue, queue processes when ready
         - Human can still intervene via `borge merge-queue cancel 123`
         - But default flow is: agent runs → submits → auto-merges

         This separation keeps Victor simple:
         - Victor = editor + visibility
         - Borge = merge orchestration  
         - Pi = isolated agent execution

         Three layers, each with one job, clean interfaces.
         |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section future-work
  (doc "Planned improvements and architectural decisions")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Parser rewrite: see sexp.borg (subsection sedlex-lexer) and
        (subsection menhir-parser). Moved there because the parser
        is a sexp library concern, not a borge top-level concern.

        Other possible future work:
        - Incremental parsing for editor integration
        - .borg language server protocol
        - Syntax highlighting definitions for common editors
      |))
  )
 )
)

